      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sunset-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Two years after the post office opened, the Fort Worth and Denver Railway ran through Sunset, turning the town into a regional rail stop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sunset-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1900 Sunset’s population had grown past 600, making it a thriving market center for the first four decades of the twentieth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sunset-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the late 1950s Sunset had 260 residents and eight businesses, but those numbers fell to 200 residents and four businesses by the late 1980s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sunset-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A Butterfield Overland Mail station once operated just two miles from Sunset, linking the town to early stagecoach routes.",
